Yes, we can absolutely make this a multi-microservice architecture, and yes, it can work in KodeKloud Kubernetes Playground, with one important condition: keep the first version cluster-local, not AWS/EKS/Terraform-heavy.
KodeKloud playground is great for hands-on Kubernetes practice, but it usually has limits around cloud provisioning, LoadBalancers, persistent cloud disks, DNS, cert-manager, and external ingress. So your app can work there, but Terraform AWS/EKS parts should be treated as a separate phase.
Recommended Playground Architecture
Build these as separate microservices:
event-ingestor
Python FastAPI/Flask API
Receives events through POST /api/events
Publishes messages to RabbitMQ

event-processor
Python worker service
Consumes RabbitMQ messages
Stores processed events in PostgreSQL

event-query-api
Python API
Reads events from PostgreSQL
Provides endpoints like /events, /events/stats, /health

dashboard
Simple Flask/React UI
Calls event-query-api
Shows event counts and recent events

notification-service
Python worker
Handles critical/high severity events
Logs alerts or simulates Slack/email notification

Infrastructure services:
PostgreSQL
RabbitMQ
Redis, optional
Prometheus
Grafana

Will This Work In KodeKloud?
Yes, if we use:
Deployment
StatefulSet
Service
ConfigMap
Secret
PVC
Ingress only if the playground supports ingress
NodePort as fallback
kubectl port-forward as the safest access method
Avoid depending on these in the playground:
AWS EKS
AWS LoadBalancer service
Route53 DNS
cert-manager TLS
Terraform creating cloud resources
ECR unless credentials are available
large persistent storage assumptions
Best Flow For Your Goal
For interview and hands-on Kubernetes expertise, I’d split the project into two environments:
Environment 1: KodeKloud Kubernetes Playground
Use this for core Kubernetes:
namespaces
deployments
services
configmaps/secrets
probes
HPA
statefulsets
rolling updates
troubleshooting
logs
exec
port-forward
network policies
Environment 2: AWS/EKS With Terraform
Use this later for cloud DevOps:
VPC
EKS
node groups
IAM
ECR
ingress controller
RDS/ElastiCache
CI/CD deployment
That gives you a better interview story:
“I first built and validated the microservices architecture on a local/playground Kubernetes cluster, then productionized the same workload on EKS using Terraform and CI/CD.”
My Recommendation
Yes, convert this into microservices, but first target this KodeKloud-friendly version:
client
  |
  v
event-ingestor
  |
  v
RabbitMQ
  |
  +--> event-processor --> PostgreSQL
  |
  +--> notification-service

dashboard --> event-query-api --> PostgreSQL

Prometheus --> scrape all services
Grafana --> dashboards
This will work well for practice and interviews. Terraform/EKS should be kept as a separate infrastructure/terraform/aws track, not required for the KodeKloud playground version.

